IMPORT prelude

-- Mercantile Law Amendment Act 1856 (Singapore) -- a written guarantee is not invalid only because its consideration is not in
-- writing (s 1); a surety or co-obligor who pays the debt or performs the duty takes over the creditor's securities and remedies, and
-- may claim indemnity from the principal debtor and a just proportion from a co-surety, co-contractor or co-debtor (s 2).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/MLAA1856.txt. The deposit says the revised edition
-- "incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; no later amendment
-- is annotated in the text. Its legislative history records the English Act 19 & 20 Vict., c. 97 (commencement 29 July 1856), applied
-- in Singapore from 12 November 1993 by the Application of English Law Act 1993 ("sections 3 and 5"), and the 1994 Revised Edition
-- (Cap. 388). The deposit's metadata records it as the current version as at 1 October 2026.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0042 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a
-- sharper question yet. The Act has three sections; this row takes both operative ones, ss 1 and 2. Not encoded: s 3 (short title);
-- whether a guarantee must be in writing at all (s 1 assumes a written, signed promise and says nothing about an oral one -- that is
-- for other law, not read here); what counts as a "proper indemnity", a "security", or a "just proportion" (taken as given facts or
-- amounts); partial payment of the debt (the text speaks only of a person who "shall pay such debt or perform such duty"); and how
-- the assigned securities are enforced.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Mercantile Law Amendment Act 1856`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `1 -- written guarantee not invalid for unwritten consideration`

-- s 1: "No special promise to be made by any person to answer for the debt, default, or miscarriage of another person, being in
-- writing, and signed by the party to be charged therewith, or some other person by him thereunto lawfully authorised, shall be
-- deemed invalid to support an action ... by reason only that the consideration for such promise does not appear in writing, or by
-- necessary inference from a written document."
--
-- "by reason only": the section removes ONE objection. Any other ground of invalidity (no consideration at all, no signature, a
-- promise that is not to answer for another) is left where other law puts it; this encoding answers only whether s 1 disposes of it.
DECLARE Promise HAS
    `is to answer for another's debt, default or miscarriage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is signed by the promisor` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is signed by a person the promisor lawfully authorised` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE Objection IS ONE OF
    `the consideration does not appear in writing`
    `no consideration was given at all`
    `the promise was never signed`
    `the promisor lacked capacity`

GIVEN `the promise` IS A Promise
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 1 covers the promise` IF
        `the promise`'s `is to answer for another's debt, default or miscarriage`
    AND `the promise`'s `is in writing`
    AND (    `the promise`'s `is signed by the promisor`
         OR  `the promise`'s `is signed by a person the promisor lawfully authorised`)

GIVEN `the promise` IS A Promise, `the objection` IS AN Objection
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 1 defeats the objection` IF
        `s 1 covers the promise` `the promise`
    AND CONSIDER `the objection`
        WHEN `the consideration does not appear in writing` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `2 -- the paying surety stands in the creditor's place`

-- s 2: "Every person who, being surety for the debt or duty of another, or being liable with another for any debt or duty, shall pay
-- such debt or perform such duty, shall be entitled to have assigned to him, or to a trustee for him, every judgment, specialty, or
-- other security which shall be held by the creditor in respect of such debt or duty, whether such judgment, specialty, or other
-- security shall or shall not be deemed at law to have been satisfied by the payment of the debt or performance of the duty".
DECLARE Payer HAS
    `is surety for another's debt or duty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is liable with another for the debt or duty` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has paid the debt or performed the duty` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 2 applies to the payer` IF
        (    `the payer`'s `is surety for another's debt or duty`
         OR  `the payer`'s `is liable with another for the debt or duty`)
    AND `the payer`'s `has paid the debt or performed the duty`

DECLARE Security HAS
    `is held by the creditor` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is held in respect of the debt or duty paid` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is deemed at law satisfied by the payment` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Whether the security is "deemed at law to have been satisfied" makes no difference: the field is read by nothing.
GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer, `the security` IS A Security
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer may have the security assigned` IF
        `s 2 applies to the payer` `the payer`
    AND `the security`'s `is held by the creditor`
    AND `the security`'s `is held in respect of the debt or duty paid`

-- s 2 (cont.): "such person shall be entitled to stand in the place of the creditor, and to use all the remedies, and, if need be,
-- and upon a proper indemnity, to use the name of the creditor, in any action or other proceeding, at law or in equity, in order to
-- obtain from the principal debtor, or any co-surety, co-contractor, or co-debtor, as the case may be, indemnification for the
-- advances made and loss sustained ... and such payment or performance so made by such surety shall not be pleadable in bar of any
-- such action or other proceeding by him".
GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer, `there is need` IS A BOOLEAN, `a proper indemnity is given to the creditor` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer may use the creditor's name` IF
        `s 2 applies to the payer` `the payer`
    AND `there is need`
    AND `a proper indemnity is given to the creditor`

GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payment cannot be pleaded in bar of the payer's action` IF
    `s 2 applies to the payer` `the payer`

-- "co-" is read as relative to the payer: a co-surety, co-contractor or co-debtor is one bound with the payer for the same debt or
-- duty (an inference; the Act does not define the words). Anyone outside the list is outside s 2.
DECLARE Defendant IS ONE OF
    `the principal debtor`
    `a co-surety`
    `a co-contractor`
    `a co-debtor`
    `a stranger to the debt`

GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer, `the defendant` IS A Defendant
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the payer may proceed against the defendant` IF
        `s 2 applies to the payer` `the payer`
    AND CONSIDER `the defendant`
        WHEN `a stranger to the debt` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

-- Proviso: "no co-surety, co-contractor, or co-debtor shall be entitled to recover from any other co-surety, co-contractor, or
-- co-debtor, by the means aforesaid, more than the just proportion to which, as between those parties themselves, such
-- last-mentioned person shall be justly liable."
--
-- The ceiling against the principal debtor is the full "advances made and loss sustained". Against a co-obligor it is the lesser of
-- that and the defendant's just proportion. Capping the co-obligor claim at the payer's own advances and loss is a reading of "in
-- order to obtain ... indemnification for the advances made and loss sustained"; the proviso itself states only the just-proportion
-- cap. The just proportion is an amount given as a fact: the Act does not say how it is fixed.
GIVEN `the payer` IS A Payer, `the defendant` IS A Defendant, `advances made` IS A NUMBER, `loss sustained` IS A NUMBER,
      `the defendant's just proportion` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most the payer may recover from the defendant` MEANS
    IF NOT `the payer may proceed against the defendant` `the payer` `the defendant`
    THEN 0
    ELSE CONSIDER `the defendant`
         WHEN `the principal debtor` THEN `advances made` PLUS `loss sustained`
         OTHERWISE `the lesser of` (`advances made` PLUS `loss sustained`) `the defendant's just proportion`
